course: dictionary
lesson: 5467
title: Context clues in a list
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A list in a sentence can explain the words inside it. «The taiga is mostly spruce, pine, fir and larch.» Spruce, pine and fir are all kinds of trees, so the list tells you that larch is probably a tree too.
explain: Test your guess by swapping in a meaning. «The taiga is mostly spruce, pine, fir and larch» still makes sense if larch means a tree, and it would make no sense if larch meant a river or a lake.
explain: A second sentence adds a clue: «snow slides off cone-shaped trees». Larch trees grow cones, so they fit that picture. When two sentences point to the same meaning, you can be more confident in your guess.
fact: A larch is a tall tree that bears cones.
fact: Larch is a conifer, but it is deciduous: its soft needles turn yellow and fall off in autumn.
fact: Deciduous means a tree that loses its leaves or needles each year, while evergreen trees keep theirs all year.
fact: Most conifers, such as pine and spruce, are evergreen.
fact: Larch wood is strong, and it is used for fences, boats and buildings.
exercise: «The taiga is mostly spruce, pine, fir and larch.» What kind of thing is a larch here? | *A tree | A kind of snow | A lake | A river
exercise: «Snow slides off cone-shaped trees.» What shape are the trees in this sentence? | Flat like a table | Round like a ball | Square | *Cone-shaped
exercise: In autumn, when does a larch lose its needles? | *In autumn | It never loses them | In spring | In summer
test: A larch is a conifer that loses its needles each autumn. A tree like this is called what? | Evergreen | Tropical | Desert | *Deciduous
test: In the phrase «larch wood», which meaning of larch is used? | A moose that wades in water | A kind of snow | *The strong wood of the tree | A cold northern lake
exam: «Far to the north lies the biggest forest on land, a dark green belt of spruce, pine, fir and larch.» Which clue tells you larch is a tree? | *The list of spruce, pine and fir around it | The word north | The word moose | The word wood
